een current time and the earliest per-benchmark started_at.\n- Given the EvalHub Server is temporarily unreachable (5xx/network), then the last successfully fetched progress remains displayed and a non-blocking inline warning Alert is visible.\n- Display matches the approved UX specification.\n\nHLR: P1-1, P1-2, P0-3 (warning display portion).\n\nBRANCH-A-E007: pre-start vs runtime failure distinction.\n\n# Pre-Start vs Runtime Failure State Distinction\n\nDescription: Distinguish jobs that failed before execution started (admission error, resource quota, scheduling failure) from jobs that ran and then failed, using status.message.message_code values enumerated by the investigation.\n\nScope: label logic rendering \"Failed (not started)\" vs \"Failed\" based on message_code classification; distinct icon/color/label per the UX specification; supplementary status.message text.\n\nAC:\n- Given a job failed before execution started (admission error indicated by message_code), then the row displays a treatment distinguishable from a runtime failure, measured by a label reflecting the failure type and distinct icon/color.\n- Classification covers the message_code values enumerated by the investigation; ambiguous or absent codes fall back to the single \"Failed\" rendering with status.message text.\n\nHLR: P2.\n\nBRANCH-B-E005: elapsed time from created_at.\n\n# Elapsed Time Display from Job Creation Timestamp\n\nDescription: Under the outcome where status.benchmarks[] is populated only at completion, display elapsed time for running jobs computed from resource.created_at available in the list endpoint, with no mid-run detail polling.\n\nScope: elapsed time = current time − resource.created_at for non-terminal jobs, rendered per the UX specification; state from the list endpoint's existing polling; no mid-run detail-polling layer.\n\nAC:\n- Given a job running for 4 minutes, then elapsed time is displayed computed from resource.created_at.\n- No requests are made to the detail endpoint for mid-run progress.\n- Display matches the approved UX specification.\n\nHLR: P1-2 (degraded form); P0-1 met by existing list polling.\n\nNow signals rationale files. Format: markdown table with Signal, Value, Rationale.\n\nLet me now write everything. First the decomposition summary body, then set frontmatter, then epics.\n\nFor frontmatter set with ai_signals nested keys: ai_signals.change_specificity=1 etc.\n\nDependencies format: \"RHAISTRAT-8005-E003\" as list — the set command takes dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E001,RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E005\"? For BRANCH-A-E006: dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E001,RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E005\". gated_by=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E001\".\n\nLet me write the decomposition summary now.\n\nAlso note: title for frontmatter of decomposition? Schema for decomposition file — let me check if there's a schema. The CLAUDE.md mentions strat-task, epic-task, decomp-review schemas. The decomposition file gets parent_strat, epic_count, critical_path_length, decompose_complete. Let me check `schema` for something like \"epic-decomposition\" — I'll just try setting fields; if the script requires a known schema it'll tell me. Actually the prompt explicitly gives the commands, so it works.\n\nLet me write the summary body now. Keep it thorough but not bloated.\n\nOne more check on epic_count semantics — I'll set epic_count=8 and clearly document: 4 main-plan + 4 branch epics. And critical_path_length=2 (main-plan; branch epics excluded per instructions).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider critical_path_length. Main plan: E001 (len 1), E002 (1), E003→E004 (2). So 2. OK.\n\nNow, writing files. The working directory: /private/var/folders/.../epic-RHAISTRAT-8005-yo4xeveb. Use absolute paths.\n\nAlso: \"No cross-references to sibling epics\" — in the summary it's fine (summary is the plan). In epic bodies, avoid E00x references. I drafted accordingly.\n\nAlso branch epic bodies: I say \"the investigation confirms...\" without IDs. Good. But 