, BRANCH-A-E007, and BRANCH-B-E005 each carry scope items and acceptance criteria that gate on the UX specification produced by E003 (\"rendered per the approved UX specification\"; \"Display patterns match the approved UX specification\"; \"The display matches the approved UX specification\"), yet none lists E003 in `dependencies` and no DAG edge exists. The decomposition itself justifies the E003 → E004 edge on exactly this basis (\"artifact → consumer ... serializing avoids rework\"), so consistency demands the same edge for the other consumers of that artifact. The summary acknowledges the dependency (\"all display epics consume the UX specification produced by E003\") but declines to encode it, citing the branch-epic dependency convention (branch `dependencies` restricted to the gating Investigation plus siblings). That convention does not create an exception to the artifact-dependency requirement, and a rule-compliant structure that expresses the edge exists — e.g., placing the display work as main-plan epics gated_by E001 with `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` (main-plan epics may list any main-plan dependency, and Rule 3 permits `gated_by` on any epic depending on an Investigation). As drawn, under branch A the display epic could be scheduled as soon as E001 and BRANCH-A-E005 resolve, before the UX specification exists, leaving its acceptance criterion unsatisfiable and recreating the rework risk the E003 → E004 edge was drawn to prevent. Per the cross-check completeness rule, a missing edge discoverable from epic content is critical even though the diagram and frontmatter are internally consistent.\n\nMinor: the branch-A mermaid diagram omits the E001 → BRANCH-A-E006 edge that is present in that epic's frontmatter `dependencies` (it is listed in the DAG Justification table and is transitively implied by E001 → AE005 → AE006, so it is not misleading, but the diagram is not a faithful rendering of the frontmatter edge set).\n\nOther checks pass: no circular