{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad88dd5c2f694b05","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing artifact-dependency edges: BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-A-E007, and\n10\t    BRANCH-B-E005 have scope items and acceptance criteria gating on the UX specification\n11\t    produced by E003 (\"per the approved UX specification\" / \"Display patterns match\n12\t    the approved UX specification\"), but none lists E003 in dependencies and no DAG\n13\t    edge exists, while E003 -> E004 was justified on exactly this artifact-to-consumer\n14\t    basis. As drawn, the branch-A display epic can start before the UX specification\n15\t    exists, leaving its AC unsatisfiable. Fix by expressing the dependency in a rule-compliant\n16\t    structure, e.g. main-plan display epics gated_by E001 with gate_failure_impact.action=remove\n17\t    (which permits dependencies on E003), so no display epic is schedulable before\n18\t    E003 delivers the spec.'\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n21\t  description: Branch-A mermaid diagram omits the E001 -> BRANCH-A-E006 edge present\n22\t    in that epic frontmatter dependencies (it is listed in the DAG Justification table\n23\t    and transitively implied by E001 -> AE005 -> AE006, so not misleading, but the\n24\t    diagram is not a faithful rendering of the frontmatter edge set).\n25\t- severity: minor\n26\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n27\t  description: 'open_questions values are internally inconsistent across Implementation\n28\t    epics in equivalent situations: E004/BRANCH-A-E007/BRANCH-B-E005 score 1 while\n29\t    BRANCH-A-E005/BRANCH-A-E006/E003 score 0 with near-identical rationales — e.g.\n30\t    BRANCH-A-E005 states no open questions change the approach (the rubric +1 condition)\n31\t    yet assigns 0, and BRANCH-A-E007 scores 1 although its visual treatment awaits\n32\t    E003 UX specification, the same pending-spec situation for which BRANCH-A-E006\n33\t    scores 0.'\n34\terror: null\n35\t---\n36\t## Review Summary\n37\t\n38\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n39\tRecommendation: revise\n40\t\n41\tOne criterion (DAG Coherence) scores 0, which forces `pass: false` under the auto-fail rule regardless of the total score. The decomposition is otherwise strong: HLR coverage, epic boundaries, type correctness, acceptance criteria, and completeness are all clean, and the conditional-branch structure around the two genuine open questions is well drawn.\n42\t\n43\t## Criterion Details\n44\t\n45\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n46\tAll seven HLRs are traced in the decomposition summary's traceability matrix and in each epic's HLR Traceability section: P0 auto-refresh (BRANCH-A-E005 detail layer + E004 list-polling AC), P0 state distinction (E004), P0 non-blocking error handling (BRANCH-A-E005 retry/preservation + BRANCH-A-E006 warning display), P1 benchmark progress (BRANCH-A-E006), P1 elapsed time (BRANCH-A-E006 / BRANCH-B-E005), P1 Page Visibility (BRANCH-A-E005), P2 failure distinction (BRANCH-A-E007 with the documented fallback as an AC on E004). Priority inheritance is correct: E001 is P0 because it gates P0 detail-polling work; E002 is P2 matching the P2 work it enables; E003 is P0 enabling the P0 state UI. No priority collapse — the P1 HLRs bundled into BRANCH-A-E006 alongside the P0 warning display are one rendering surface, not independently deferrable features. The branch-B degradation of P0-1/P0-3 is inherent to the strategy's open question and is explicitly flagged as a health warning rather than silently dropped.\n47\t\n48\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n49\t**Critical — missing artifact-dependency edges from E003 to the display branch epics.** BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-A-E007, and BRANCH-B-E005 each carry scope items and acceptance criteria that gate on the UX specification produced by E003 (\"rendered per the approved UX specification\"; \"Display patterns match the approved UX specification\"; \"The display matches the approved UX specification\"), yet none lists E003 in `dependencies` and no DAG edge exists. The decomposition itself justifies the E003 → E004 edge on exactly this basis (\"artifact → consumer ... serializing avoids rework\"), so consistency demands the same edge for the other consumers of that artifact. The summary acknowledges the dependency (\"all display epics consume the UX specification produced by E003\") but declines to encode it, citing the branch-epic dependency convention (branch `dependencies` restricted to the gating Investigation plus siblings). That convention does not create an exception to the artifact-dependency requirement, and a rule-compliant structure that expresses the edge exists — e.g., placing the display work as main-plan epics gated_by E001 with `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` (main-plan epics may list any main-plan dependency, and Rule 3 permits `gated_by` on any epic depending on an Investigation). As drawn, under branch A the display epic could be scheduled as soon as E001 and BRANCH-A-E005 resolve, before the UX specification exists, leaving its acceptance criterion unsatisfiable and recreating the rework risk the E003 → E004 edge was drawn to prevent. Per the cross-check completeness rule, a missing edge discoverable from epic content is critical even though the diagram and frontmatter are internally consistent.\n50\t\n51\tMinor: the branch-A mermaid diagram omits the E001 → BRANCH-A-E006 edge that is present in that epic's frontmatter `dependencies` (it is listed in the DAG Justification table and is transitively implied by E001 → AE005 → AE006, so it is not misleading, but the diagram is not a faithful rendering of the frontmatter edge set).\n52\t\n53\tOther checks pass: no circular dependencies; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies`; every branch file sets both `branch` and `gated_by`, and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`; the main-plan critical path (2) matches the S-size heuristic and branch epics are excluded from it by convention; the branch convention restrictions that were honored (gating Investigation + siblings only) are otherwise respected; no konflux/license-validation/repo-onboarding epics exist, so the mandated-parallelism rules are not applicable.\n54\t\n55\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n56\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: TrustyAI / EvalHub investigations (E001, E002), UX/Design specification (E003), ODH Dashboard implementation (E004, all branch epics). No epic spans multiple teams. No scope duplication: E004 (state badges) and BRANCH-A-E007 (failure classification on top of those badges) are layered, not overlapping; the branch-A and branch-B elapsed-time epics are mutually exclusive branches, not duplicates. Work-product types are correctly separated (UX spec authoring in E003 vs. code implementation elsewhere). The passive odh-dashboard BFF validation is correctly an acceptance criterion on BRANCH-A-E005 rather than a separate epic. All epics are well under ~2 weeks.\n57\t\n58\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n59\tBoth Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream structure: E001 selects branch A vs. branch B for the progress-display work; E002 selects BRANCH-A-E007 vs. the E004 fallback AC. Both have downstream gated dependents. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. All four branch epics have `gated_by` set with `gate_failure_impact` carrying both `action` and a substantive `fallback_approach`; every epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set; every `gated_by` target is in the epic's direct `dependencies`. E002's branch-B outcome (no branch epic, fallback AC on E004) is a legitimate bounded-outcome treatment.\n60\t\n61\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n62\tType-appropriate signal fields are used throughout (investigation_signals on E001/E002 with ai_signals null; ai_signals on all six Implementation epics with investigation_signals null), and all eight signal-rationale files exist. The Investigation signal values are consistent with the 5-signal rubric and their rationales. However, the `open_questions` values are assigned inconsistently across Implementation epics in equivalent situations: E004, BRANCH-A-E007, and BRANCH-B-E005 score 1 (\"all design decisions resolved\") while BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-A-E006, and E003 score 0, with rationales that read nearly identically — e.g., BRANCH-A-E005's rationale states the gating question is resolved before start and the residual risk \"does not change the approach\" (the rubric's +1 condition) yet assigns 0, while BRANCH-A-E007's identical situation (\"gating question ... resolved by the investigation before this branch epic starts\") assigns 1; conversely E004 and BRANCH-A-E007 score 1 although their visual treatments await E003's UX specification, the same pending-spec situation for which BRANCH-A-E006 scores 0. Each individual value has a defensible reading, so this is arguable rather than a flat rubric contradiction, but the set is not internally consistent.\n63\t\n64\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n65\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy, and the strategy's seven proposed acceptance criteria are faithfully distributed (state distinction, 30s auto-refresh, benchmark X/N, elapsed time, queued label, failure distinction, error-tolerance, and background-tab pause all appear as epic ACs). No rule-mandated AC applies (no replacement/rollback work — the change is additive per the strategy's backwards-compatibility NFR; no konflux chain; Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not Yes, so no docs-authoring AC is required). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — cross-references use full epic IDs in frontmatter or descriptive names in prose.\n66\t\n67\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n68\tThe full strategy scope is covered: two-layer polling, round-robin concurrency cap, progress model, retry/backoff with last-success preservation, Page Visibility pause/resume, state differentiation, BFF passthrough verification (as an AC), and the fallback renderings. Conditional branches cover all bounded outcomes of both Investigations (E001: branch A / branch B; E002: branch A epic / documented fallback AC on E004). Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than dropped: the single-benchmark risk mitigation (elapsed time as primary indicator) appears in BRANCH-A-E006 and BRANCH-B-E005; the message_code ambiguity risk is the E004/AE007 fallback; the Module Federation validation risk is an AC on BRANCH-A-E005; the deferred-scalability and pending-gate items are surfaced as health warnings. The staff-engineer overrides (no new API data, visible-page-only polling, 5-concurrent round-robin, 10s sufficiency) are traced and incorporated. No docs-authoring epic is required since Documentation Support is not listed as Yes.\n69\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83c3fc0004f5a67f","agent_id":"a0ccc90171ba1f7fa","session_id":"4d1cfcd2-7a48-4a97-8f21-5ac7a29e76ba","uuid":"3c612191-5329-4317-87e5-ec0cee326558","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:44:58.123Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad88dd5c2f694b05","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8005 decomposition"}
