{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-901c4152f2b4d324","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8005 — EvalHub UI: Real-Time Evaluation Job Progress and State Visibility\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Outcome\n13\t\n14\tThe strategy is S-sized, single active component (eval-hub-ui plugin), effectively single implementing team (ODH Dashboard). However, it contains two explicit open questions flagged \"needs validation\" in the strategy's Assumptions and Open Questions tables, and each answer changes which downstream epics exist or what they do (mid-run detail polling and benchmark-progress display hinge on whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run; the P2 failure distinction hinges on `status.message.message_code` values). The genuine-unknowns escape condition applies — full decomposition with Investigation epics proceeds.\n15\t\n16\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5 traceability)\n17\t\n18\tThe Staff Engineer Input takes precedence over the AI-generated Technical Approach. The following directives were incorporated:\n19\t\n20\t1. **No new API data**: The plan assumes no data beyond what the existing list (`GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs`) and detail (`GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id}`) endpoints currently provide. No server-side changes; out-of-scope items reinforced.\n21\t2. **List vs. detail field inventory**: The Staff field table (description, tags, `status.benchmarks[]`, mlflow_run_id, logs_path, per-benchmark parameters/pass_criteria available only in the detail response) is authoritative for the progress model.\n22\t3. **Visible-page-only polling**: Only jobs on the currently displayed table page (client-side pagination, default 20 rows, options 5/10/20 per `tablePaginationConstants.ts`) are detail-polled; page turns retarget polling immediately.\n23\t4. **Concurrency**: Maximum 5 parallel detail fetches, round-robin (next job dispatched as one completes); the 10-second timer starts only when all calls for the current page complete.\n24\t5. **Retry pattern**: Replicate the autorag/automl React Query retry-with-exponential-backoff pattern; non-blocking warning while last successful status remains displayed.\n25\t6. **Resolved questions**: 10-second latency is sufficient; Page Visibility API pause confirmed; scalability deferred for this release (all user polling behavior identical).\n26\t\n27\tTwo unknowns remain open after the Staff review (both listed in the strategy's Open Questions table, owner TrustyAI / EvalHub team) and become Investigation epics.\n28\t\n29\t## Component Name Mappings (canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`)\n30\t\n31\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Notes |\n32\t| --- | --- | --- |\n33\t| eval-hub-ui (odh-dashboard plugin) | AI Evaluations | Closest canonical name for the EvalHub UI work |\n34\t| EvalHub Server API (data-availability questions) | AI Evaluations | Investigation subject |\n35\t| odh-dashboard (BFF) | AI Evaluations (passive) | No code change expected; same owning team as the active component, so BFF field-passthrough validation is an acceptance criterion on the detail-polling epic, not a separate epic |\n36\t| Dashboard design system (UX/Design) | UXD | UX specification epic |\n37\t\n38\t## Epic List\n39\t\n40\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n41\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 | Investigation: mid-run population of `status.benchmarks[]` in the detail endpoint | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 | Investigation: `status.message.message_code` values for pre-start vs. runtime failures | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P2 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 | UX specification for evaluation job state and progress indicators | Implementation | UX/Design | P0 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E004 | Evaluation job state visual differentiation in the EvalHub UI | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E005 | Detail-polling data layer for visible-page evaluation jobs (gated by E001, outcome A) | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n47\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E006 | Benchmark progress, elapsed time, and polling warning display (gated by E001, outcome A) | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n48\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E007 | Pre-start vs. runtime failure state distinction (gated by E002, outcome A) | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P2 |\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E008 | Elapsed time display from job creation timestamp (gated by E001, outcome B) | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P1 |\n50\t\n51\tE005–E008 are main-plan epics whose existence is conditional on an Investigation outcome, expressed via `gated_by` plus `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` in their frontmatter: E005/E006 exist only in the outcome where `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run; E008 exists only in the opposite outcome (E005/E006 and E008 are mutually exclusive alternatives); E007 exists only if `message_code` reliably distinguishes failure types. This structure (rather than conditional branch files, whose dependency convention cannot express the UX-specification artifact edges) allows every display epic to list the UX specification epic as a direct dependency.\n52\t\n53\t## Dependency DAG\n54\t\n55\t```mermaid\n56\tgraph TD\n57\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: benchmarks[] mid-run population\"]\n58\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: message_code failure values\"]\n59\t    E003[\"E003 UX specification for state and progress indicators\"]\n60\t    E004[\"E004 Evaluation job state visual differentiation UI\"]\n61\t    E005[\"E005 Detail-polling data layer\"]\n62\t    E006[\"E006 Progress, elapsed time, warning display\"]\n63\t    E007[\"E007 Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction\"]\n64\t    E008[\"E008 Elapsed time display from created_at\"]\n65\t    E003 --> E004\n66\t    E001 --> E005\n67\t    E001 --> E006\n68\t    E001 --> E008\n69\t    E002 --> E007\n70\t    E003 --> E006\n71\t    E003 --> E007\n72\t    E003 --> E008\n73\t    E005 --> E006\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\t### Conditional (gated) epics\n77\t\n78\tThe two Investigations have bounded outcomes that change which downstream epics exist. The outcome-conditional work is expressed as main-plan epics with `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact`:\n79\t\n80\t- **E001 outcome A** (`status.benchmarks[]` populated while a job is running — detail polling has mid-run value): E005 (detail-polling data layer) and E006 (benchmark progress and elapsed time display) proceed.\n81\t- **E001 outcome B** (`status.benchmarks[]` populated only after completion): E005 and E006 are removed; E008 (elapsed time from `resource.created_at`, no mid-run detail requests) proceeds instead.\n82\t- **E002 outcome A** (`message_code` values reliably distinguish pre-start from runtime failures): E007 proceeds.\n83\t- **E002 outcome B** (does not reliably distinguish): E007 is removed; the strategy's documented fallback (single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text as supplementary detail) is an acceptance criterion on E004.\n84\t\n85\t## DAG Justification\n86\t\n87\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n88\t| --- | --- | --- |\n89\t| E003 → E004, E006, E007, E008 | 12/13 (artifact → consumer) | The state UI and every display epic build against the UX specification's icon/color/label decisions; serializing avoids rework. Each of these epics has scope items or acceptance criteria referencing the approved UX specification, so each lists E003 in `dependencies`. |\n90\t| E001 → E005, E006, E008 | 3 (Investigation gate, bounded outcome) | Whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run decides whether the detail-polling layer and benchmark-progress display exist (outcome A) or degrade to elapsed-time-only from list data (outcome B). Recorded as `gated_by` E001 with `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. |\n91\t| E002 → E007 | 3 (Investigation gate, bounded outcome) | Whether `message_code` values distinguish pre-start from runtime failures decides whether the P2 distinction feature exists; fallback (single \"Failed\" + message text) is an AC on E004. |\n92\t| E005 → E006 | 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The display components consume the polling hook's data and error state. |\n93\t| No edge E001/E002 ↔ E003/E004 | 14 (independent) | State differentiation from the list endpoint's `status.state` is independent of both data-availability unknowns and of the UX spec timeline. |\n94\t| No edge between E001 and E002 | 14 | Independent unknowns, different questions and deliverables; combining them would create a 4-outcome branch matrix. |\n95\t| No edge E004 ↔ E005/E006/E007/E008 | 14 (independent) | The state badge rendering and the progress/elapsed/failure-distinction rendering are separate surfaces in the same table; neither produces an artifact the other consumes. |\n96\t| No edge from BFF validation | Passive component rule | odh-dashboard BFF needs no code change; same owning team as the active component → acceptance criterion on the detail-polling epic (E005). |\n97\t| No `konflux-onboarding` / `repo-onboarding` epics | 20 | eval-hub-ui plugin already onboarded; work happens in the existing odh-dashboard repo. |\n98\t\n99\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n100\t\n101\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n102\t| --- | --- | --- |\n103\t| Auto-refresh mechanism (list polling for top-level state + detail polling for progress) | P0 | E005 (new detail-polling layer); top-level state auto-refresh is existing 30s list-endpoint polling, verified as an AC on E004. Under E001 outcome B, this HLR is met by the existing list polling (no new detail polling has mid-run value). |\n104\t| Visual distinction of queued/running/failed/completed states | P0 | E004 |\n105\t| Non-blocking error handling during detail polling | P0 | E005 (retry/backoff, last-success preservation), E006 (inline warning display) |\n106\t| Benchmark-level progress display (X/N) | P1 | E006 (E001 outcome A only; not implementable mid-run under outcome B — see the gated-epic structure) |\n107\t| Elapsed time display | P1 | E006 (from per-benchmark `started_at`), E008 (from `resource.created_at` under outcome B) |\n108\t| Page Visibility pause/resume of polling | P1 | E005 (scoped to detail polling per the strategy's technical approach; moot under outcome B where no mid-run detail polling exists) |\n109\t| Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction | P2 | E007 (outcome A of the failure-code investigation); fallback single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text is an AC on E004 (outcome B) |\n110\t\n111\tAll seven HLRs are covered in every outcome branch.\n112\t\n113\t## Health Warnings\n114\t\n115\t- **P0 scope is contingent on E001**: the only new P0 work under outcome B is state differentiation (E004); P0-1/P0-3/P1-3 all attach to the detail-polling layer that exists only under outcome A (E005/E006). Under outcome B the strategy delivers substantially less than its P0 list implies, using existing list-polling behavior (E008 only). PM/Engineering validation of the proposed acceptance criteria (flagged \"requires PM/Engineering validation\" in the strategy) should confirm this is acceptable.\n116\t- **Unresolved human process gates**: Architecture Review, License Validation, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy's prerequisites table. These are human gates on the implementation epics, not decomposition blockers.\n117\t- **Performance review risk**: the Performance Team's polling-load assessment is pending while the strategy explicitly defers scalability for this release. If that review later rejects the 5-concurrent-request cap or visible-page-only scoping, the gated detail-polling scope (E005/E006) is rewritten.\n118\t- **Module Federation validation risk** (strategy risk table): the autorag React Qu